// Windows process entry and boot sequence (#3330, epic #3322): the PE
// `AddressOfEntryPoint` function, the main thread's identity token, and
// `boot` — bring the collector and scheduler up, run the user's `bit_main`
// as the first green thread, then exit with its status. The Bit form of
// `../linux/boot.bit`'s and `../darwin/boot.bit`'s `boot`, for the shape this
// header explains rather than repeats.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// READ ../darwin/boot.bit's HEADER FIRST — this is the same shape, not linux's
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Windows is the closer analogue to Darwin, not Linux: the loader has already
// run and the process is initialized by the time `_start` (below) gets
// control — there is no `_start`-style raw kernel stack to walk, no argc/
// argv/envp handed in registers, and no auxiliary vector. Where Darwin's
// entry differs from Linux's in exactly two places (the entry ABI and no
// auxv/static-TLS install), Windows differs from BOTH platforms in a third:
// there is no per-process argc/argv/envp AT ALL. A Windows program obtains
// its command line via `GetCommandLineW` and its environment via
// `GetEnvironmentStringsW` — both UTF-16, neither shaped like the C
// `envp: [*:0]?[*:0]u8` array `../../root/os.bit`'s shared `osSetArgs`/
// `gcEnvValuePtr` machinery already assumes.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO YET, AND WHY THAT IS SOUND RATHER THAN A GAP
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Six sibling Stage B tickets (epic #3322) were opened BECAUSE the pieces
// below were real, separate modules this file could not reach into when it
// was first written — none of `runtime/root/windows/io.bit` (#3331),
// `os.bit` (#3332), `time.bit` (#3333), `runtime/thread/windows` (#3335),
// `runtime/park/windows` (#3336), `runtime/root/windows/signal.bit` (#3337)
// existed yet. All six now do; the gateway design let them land
// independently on top of this file, exactly as ../linux and ../darwin's own
// io/os/time/signal siblings sit beside their boot.bit today. The bullets
// below record each one's landing status.
//
//   - REAL ARGV/ENVP: DONE (#3332). `./os.bit`'s `winCaptureArgsEnv` now
//     captures `GetCommandLineW`/`GetEnvironmentStringsW` and publishes them
//     through `../../root/os.bit`'s `osSetArgs`, so `rootConfigureFromEnv`/
//     `rootEnvWorkers` below see real BIT_GC/BIT_WORKERS overrides, same as
//     ../linux and ../darwin. See "EXACTLY ONE WORKER" below for what that
//     changes.
//   - FATAL SIGNAL / VECTORED EXCEPTION HANDLING: DONE (#3337). A process-
//     wide `AddVectoredExceptionHandler` (installed at step 1b below) maps
//     an access violation, an unattributed native stack overflow, and the
//     #2432/#2946 `ud2` trap to the same Bit-visible panics ../linux and
//     ../darwin produce. `mainStackBaseAddr` (set in step 5) feeds it, same
//     as the other two platforms. Unlike them, no alternate-stack mapping is
//     needed — see ./signal.bit's header for why AddVectoredExceptionHandler
//     does not need one.
//   - THE NETPOLLER ITSELF: DONE (#3339). `../../sched/windows/poll.bit`
//     translates IOCP's completion model onto the readiness-shaped
//     `pollCreate`/`pollClose`/`pollRegister`/`pollDrain` contract
//     ../linux/poll.bit and ../darwin/poll.bit already present, and step 4b/
//     8b below and `workerBody`'s find-work loop bind it in exactly the
//     shape ../linux and ../darwin's own boot files do.
//   - SOCKETS: DONE (#3340). `runtime/net/windows/{sock,tcp,udp,netabi}.bit`
//     implement AcceptEx/ConnectEx/WSARecv/WSASend on top of this poller, so
//     the completion port this file creates now has handles to register.
//   - `BIT_GC_STATS` REPORT: DONE (#3725). `./stats.bit`'s `statsReport()`
//     writes through this file's own `rootWriteAll`/`writeConst` (`./io.bit`,
//     landed by #3331), and step 9 below calls it gated on `rootEnvVerbose`,
//     same as ../linux and ../darwin.
//   - EXACTLY ONE WORKER BY DEFAULT, BIT_WORKERS>1 NOW EFFICIENT: DONE
//     (#3336). `rootEnvWorkers(1)` is still called unchanged, but
//     `workerBody` below now matches ../linux and ../darwin's own split:
//     worker 0 keeps polling (it drives the timer queue and, once Stage C
//     lands, the netpoller), workers 1..N-1 arm and park on
//     `schedIdlePark`/`WaitOnAddress` (`./../../park/windows`, #3336) instead
//     of busy-waiting. `parkMonoNs`/`parkSleepNs` also moved here from this
//     file's own placeholder `winMonoNs`/`winSleepNs`, which raced under a
//     real multi-worker pool (see their old comments) — the park provider's
//     caller-owned-scratch shape closes that.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE WIN64 ENTRY ABI
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The PE loader calls `AddressOfEntryPoint` on the ordinary Win64 convention
// (32-byte home space, RCX/RDX/R8/R9, RSI/RDI/XMM6-15 callee-saved —
// `compiler/x64call.bit`'s `XCallConv.Win64`) with a valid, aligned frame
// already set up, exactly as Darwin's `LC_MAIN` does — but with NO
// parameters at all: there is no CRT here to have parsed argc/argv first, so
// the entry is the bare `void WinMainCRTStartup(void)` shape. `winMain`
// below takes none. The compiler is responsible for selecting Win64 as this
// object's calling convention and for the prologue/epilogue that follows from
// it — this file writes ordinary Bit, not hand-encoded frame setup, the same
// division of labour ../darwin/boot.bit's `machoMain` already relies on.
//
// `compiler/pelink.bit:67`'s `plEntrySymbol()` names the PE entry point by
// the literal symbol `_start` (the linker designates it BY NAME, exactly as
// ABI.md §9 documents for Mach-O's `_start` pin), which is why `winMain`
// below carries the same `@symbol("_start")` pin ../darwin/boot.bit's
// `machoMain` does — one pinned name, two different platform objects, never
// linked into the same program.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE MAIN THREAD'S IDENTITY TOKEN — NOW A NON-ISSUE, AND HERE IS WHY (#3412)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file used to install a per-thread token itself: `gcThreadToken()` was
// platform-free, an unconditional `movq %fs:0, %rax`, and Windows starts every
// thread — the main one included — with FS_BASE at 0, so this file WRFSBASE'd
// a self-referential word onto FS_BASE before anything could reach the
// collector. #3410/#3391 then measured, on real hardware, that a
// WRFSBASE-installed FS_BASE does NOT survive a Windows context switch
// (non-zero immediately before a deschedule, `0000000000000000` after) — so
// that install was never actually safe past a worker's first park, only past
// its first few instructions.
//
// #3420 removed the need for any install at all: `runtime/gc/gcthread.bit`
// now declares `gcThreadToken()`'s body as a per-OS extern
// (`bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token`), and `runtime/gc/windows/gcthread.bit`'s
// implementation reads the TEB self-pointer at `gs:[0x30]` (`NT_TIB.Self`)
// instead of `fs:0`. That value is OS-allocated before a thread's first
// instruction runs and is restored by Windows itself on every context switch
// — the same guarantee `GetLastError`/SEH/FLS/TLS already depend on — so it
// needs no install call, for the main thread or any `CreateThread`'d worker.
// See that file's header for the full decision record and the hardware
// measurements behind it.
//
// So this file has NOTHING left to do for thread identity. What used to be
// `winInstallMainThreadToken`/`winSetFsBase` here is deleted, not kept as a
// second, unused path documenting the disproven WRFSBASE design — see
// ../../thread/windows/spawn.bit's header for the worker-side half of the
// same cleanup.

import {
  schedInit, schedTaskInit, schedRegister, schedSpawn, schedShutdown,
  schedWorkerAt, taskAt, taskWords, schedStackBytes, schedMainStackBytes,
  schedStackAligned, schedRegisterBigStack, wkDeque, schedTaskExit, dqPushBottom,
  WORKER_MAX_STEPS, workerStepStop, scStopping, schedFindWorkAt,
  schedWorkerStepAt, wkLastDone, taskStackBase, taskStackBytes,
  taskSpawnEntry, wkSleepReq,
  schedIdleArm, schedIdleDisarm, schedIdlePark, schedSetPollFd,
} from "../../sched"
// The netpoller provider (#3339) and the spawn trampoline (#3503). `../../sched`
// may not import either: IOCP against epoll/kqueue is the platform split the
// parent module exists to stay clear of, and the trampoline's Win64 argument
// register is exactly the OS-conditional choice `../../sched/task.bit`'s own
// header says its `asm` cannot express (see `../../sched/windows/spawn.bit`'s
// header for the full argument). Both bindings are made here, in the leaf
// that is already per-platform, exactly as ../linux/boot.bit and
// ../darwin/boot.bit bind their own netpoller twins at the same three points.
import { pollCreate, pollClose, pollDrain, winSpawnTrampoline } from "../../sched/windows"
// `wkId`/`schedCurrentWorker`/`schedMaxWorkers` (from "../../sched") are
// already imported into this module by ./io.bit — an import is per-MODULE,
// not per-file, so a second import of any of them here would be E0042.
import { threadStackBytes } from "../../thread"
import { parkMinBackoffNs, parkMaxBackoffNs, parkNextBackoff } from "../../park"
import { parkMonoNs, parkSleepNs, parkWait, parkWake } from "../../park/windows"
import { mapPages, unmapPages, protectPages, guardPages } from "../../alloc/windows"
import {
  rootBindMemory, rootInit, rootConfigureFromEnv, rootEnvMarkStack,
  rootEnvWorkers, rootEnvVerbose,
} from "../../root"
import { rootGcAddr, rootWorldAddr } from "../../root"
import { chanBootBind } from "../../chan"
import {
  gcBlockingBegin,
  gcBlockingEnd,
  gcThreadEnter,
  gcThreadExit,
  gcDefaultMinTrigger,
  gcDefaultMarkStack,
  gcDefaultGrowthPct,
} from "../../gc"
import { stwBind } from "../../stw"

// The per-program entry the compiled user object defines (ABI.md §10),
// exactly as ../linux and ../darwin's own `extern fn bit_main` — resolved to
// `U bit_main` at final program link.
extern fn bit_main(): i32

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// kernel32 imports (compiler/pesyms.bit's admitted surface, #3328) — every
// name below is already in that table.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

extern fn ExitProcess(code: i32)
extern fn CreateThread(
  attrs: *byte, stackSize: i64, startAddr: *byte,
  param: *byte, flags: i32, threadIdOut: *i32,
): *byte
// `WaitForSingleObject` here is ONLY for joining a worker's real CreateThread
// HANDLE (step 8 below) — the monotonic-clock and idle-sleep declarations
// this file used to carry (`QueryPerformanceCounter`/`_Frequency`,
// `GetCurrentProcess`) moved to ../../park/windows/wait.bit (#3336) along
// with the `winMonoNs`/`winSleepNs` bodies that used them.
extern fn WaitForSingleObject(handle: *byte, timeoutMs: i32): i32
extern fn CloseHandle(handle: *byte): i32
// Raises the system-wide timer tick from its default ~15.6ms to 1ms for this
// process's lifetime (#3336; Windows 10 2004+ scopes a period request to the
// requesting process and undoes it automatically on exit, so no matching
// `timeEndPeriod` is needed for a process that never returns it early).
// Without this, every `../../park/windows/wait.bit` WaitForSingleObject or
// WaitOnAddress timeout is quantized to the system tick — measured on
// mustafa-desktop-win: a 2000-iteration 1ms sleep loop took 31.5s instead of
// ~2s. Returns a UINT (TIMERR_NOERROR == 0 on success); discarded here the
// same way this file already discards CreateThread's non-fatal siblings,
// since a failure only costs precision, not correctness.
extern fn timeBeginPeriod(periodMs: i32): i32

// CreateThread's dwCreationFlags: interpret dwStackSize as the RESERVE size
// (not the initial commit), so every worker gets the same 16MiB budget
// ../../thread's `threadStackBytes` already names for the other platforms,
// rather than whatever SizeOfStackReserve the PE header happens to carry.
const STACK_SIZE_PARAM_IS_A_RESERVATION: i32 = 0x00010000

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Boot-time storage, sized for `bootMaxWorkers` — identical layout constants
// to ../linux and ../darwin (see their comments for why the sizes are
// literals, not derived: an array size must be a literal, so
// `[workerWords * bootMaxWorkers]` does not parse).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
let schedBlock: [16]i64
let workerBlock: [9344]i64
let mainTaskBlock: [32]i64

// One CreateThread HANDLE per started worker — this file's own join state.
// Unlike ../linux/../darwin's `startDones`/`startHandles` pair, there is no
// separate "done word": WaitForSingleObject on the real OS thread handle
// below IS the join, natively bounded by a timeout, so nothing here needs a
// manually-polled completion flag.
let startHandles: [32]i64

// CreateThread's `lpThreadId` out-param: no caller reads it (the HANDLE, not
// the id, is what `boot` joins on below), but the parameter must be a valid
// address. Module state, not a local — `ptrOf` addresses a cell (§11.11).
// Safe to share across the spawn loop's calls: each CreateThread overwrites
// it before returning, and nothing reads it after.
let winThreadIdScratch: i32 = 0

let mainExitCode: int = 0
let mainDone: i32 = 0

// Published for `vehHandler` (#3337, ./signal.bit) to name a main-task stack
// overflow exactly rather than by heuristic — set once, in step 5 below,
// before `bit_main`'s task is ever queued. Same role as ../linux and
// ../darwin's own `mainStackBaseAddr`.
let mainStackBaseAddr: int = 0

// The netpoller descriptor `boot` creates (#3339), kept so teardown can close
// it after the join — see step 4b and step 8b below, and ../linux/boot.bit's
// identically-named field for the full ordering argument.
let bootPollFd: int = 0
let gSched: int = 0

let chanReserveDesc: [3]i64
let chanRegBlock: [2]i64

const arenaWords: int = 1048576
const indexWords: int = 16384
const pageSize: int = 4096
const bootMaxWorkers: int = 32
const joinTimeoutNs: int = 15000000000
const chanReserveBytes: int = 1048576
// `main`'s own stack size is `schedMainStackBytes` (`../../sched`, imported
// above) — see that const's own comment for why 8 MiB, and why masking
// cannot name a stack this size, so it is named through `schedRegisterBigStack`
// instead. No local copy: ../darwin and ../linux each import the same const.

// Stack alignment + the guarded-stack allocator (`alignUpStack`,
// `alignDownStack`, `mapGuardHole`, `guardedStackBase`, `mapGuardedStack`)
// moved to ./bootstack.bit (#3818), to keep this file under the 800-line
// E0200 limit once ./bootstack.bit's own header explains why. Same module,
// same directory (an import is per-module, not per-file) — nothing else
// here changes.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The boot thread's own clock/sleep scratch — see ../linux/boot.bit's
// identically-named `bootSleepReq`. The boot thread (this file's `boot()`,
// step 7 below) is not a scheduler worker and so has no per-worker
// `wkSleepReq` to reach; a dedicated module cell is safe because only this
// one thread ever touches it (the same premise ../linux/boot.bit's own
// `bootSleepReq` relies on).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

let bootSleepReq: [2]i64

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The worker OS-thread entry — now the SAME worker-0-polls-the-rest-park
// split ../linux and ../darwin's own `workerBody` uses (#3336). See
// runtime/sched/sleep.bit's header for the full argument (a per-worker park
// word, armed before the find-work pass, claimed by a producer with a CAS);
// this function only wires that machinery in, it does not re-derive it.
//
// `arg` is the worker's own address directly (`schedWorkerAt(sched, wi)`,
// passed as CreateThread's `lpParameter`) — no {workerAddr, doneAddr} bundle
// like the other platforms build via `schedStartArm`: this file's join is
// WaitForSingleObject on the real CreateThread HANDLE in `boot` below, so
// there is no manually-polled "done word" for this thread to clear.
//
// NO USERSPACE SPIN (#3260/#3264): unlike ../linux and ../darwin, this loop
// parks after exactly one failed find-work pass. Deliberately left out —
// it is a pure latency/CPU-tradeoff optimisation on top of a correct park,
// not something this ticket's acceptance (idle CPU, ticker accuracy, no
// lost wakeup, no drift) requires, and adding it means also porting
// `spinForWork`'s bookkeeping, which is a separate, later change.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn workerBody(arg: *byte): *byte {
  gcThreadEnter()
  // This OS thread's own stack-overflow guarantee (#3337, ./signal.bit) —
  // per-thread state; the boot thread's own call at step 1b does not cover
  // this CreateThread'd thread.
  winInstallStackGuarantee()

  let w = taskAt(int(arg))
  let s: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBlock)
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  // WORKER 0 POLLS, THE REST PARK (#1902 on ../linux/../darwin; #3336 here):
  // worker 0 is the one that still drives the timer queue every turn (see
  // `schedFindWorkAt`'s own comment on `schedTimerExpire`). `pollDrain`
  // below (#3339) is called by EVERY idle worker regardless of `parks`,
  // matching ../linux and ../darwin exactly — `schedPollTryEnter`'s try-lock
  // is what arbitrates, not this split; the split only decides who parks
  // between drains.
  let parks = *(w + wkId) != 0
  let n = 0
  while (n < WORKER_MAX_STEPS) {
    n = n + 1
    // ARM BEFORE LOOKING FOR WORK, never after — see runtime/sched/sleep.bit's
    // header for why the order is the correctness argument.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleArm(w)
    }
    let addr = schedFindWorkAt(w, parkMonoNs(w + wkSleepReq))
    // Non-blocking drain (timeout 0) — a worker must never sit inside
    // `GetQueuedCompletionStatusEx` where it cannot answer the collector's
    // stop-the-world rendezvous. See ../../sched/windows/poll.bit's
    // `pollDrain` header.
    let woke = 0
    if (addr == 0) {
      woke = pollDrain(int(s), 0)
    }
    if (addr == 0 && woke == 0) {
      if (atomicLoad(s + scStopping) != 0) {
        break
      }
      gcBlockingBegin()
      if (parks) {
        schedIdlePark(s, w)
      } else {
        parkSleepNs(backoff, w + wkSleepReq)
      }
      gcBlockingEnd()
      backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
      continue
    }
    // Running instead of parking: clear the arm so a producer does not
    // spend its one wake on a worker that is already busy.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleDisarm(w)
    }
    backoff = schedWorkerStepAt(w, addr, woke, backoff)
    let doneTask = *(w + wkLastDone)
    if (doneTask != 0 && doneTask != int(ptrOf(mainTaskBlock))) {
      let dt = taskAt(doneTask)
      unmapPages(*(dt + taskStackBase), *(dt + taskStackBytes))
      unmapPages(doneTask, (taskWords + 1) * 8)
    }
    if (backoff == workerStepStop) {
      break
    }
  }

  gcThreadExit()
  return nil
}

// The boot thread's step-7 wait for `main`, placed here beside
// `mainTrampoline`, the only writer of `mainDone`. See
// ../darwin/bootworker.bit's twin for the full argument; the three points that
// decide correctness are:
//
//   - #3717: this was an open-coded `parkSleepNs` ramp capped at
//     `parkMaxBackoffNs`, so once it saturated the thread woke 1000 times a
//     second for the life of the process. `parkWait` is a COMPARE-and-wait
//     (`WaitOnAddress` here), so a store landing between this thread's own load
//     and the wait is caught by the kernel's own compare, not missed.
//   - THE TIMEOUT IS 0 — WAIT FOREVER — matching the other two platforms, where
//     a finite one is barred by ../linux's heap-allocating `timespec` inside
//     the `gcBlockingBegin` bracket. Here 0 maps to INFINITE.
//   - THE RE-CHECK IS THE SPIN GUARD, and this is the platform that needs it
//     most: ../../park/windows/wait.bit's `parkWait` returns `parkWaitReady`
//     WITHOUT BLOCKING when its 32-slot scratch pool is full, and
//     `WaitOnAddress` documents spurious returns outright. One
//     `parkMaxBackoffNs` sleep makes the worst case the 1ms poll this replaced,
//     and never worse.
//
// NOT VERIFIED ON WINDOWS (#3717): the change was written and gated on
// aarch64-macos, where the darwin twin is measured and mutation-tested. This
// copy compiles for x86_64-windows and is textually identical in shape, but no
// Windows execution backs it.
fn bootWaitForMain() {
  while (atomicLoad(ptrOf(mainDone)) == i32(0)) {
    gcBlockingBegin()
    let _ = parkWait(ptrOf(mainDone), i32(0), 0)
    if (atomicLoad(ptrOf(mainDone)) == i32(0)) {
      parkSleepNs(parkMaxBackoffNs, ptrOf(bootSleepReq))
    }
    gcBlockingEnd()
  }
}

// The first green thread: run the user's `bit_main`, publish its exit code,
// leave the task. Identical to ../linux and ../darwin's `mainTrampoline`.
fn mainTrampoline() {
  mainExitCode = int(bit_main())
  atomicStore(ptrOf(mainDone), i32(1))
  // #3717: the boot thread is PARKED on this word in `bootWaitForMain` above,
  // not polling it. STRICTLY AFTER the store — `parkWait`'s compare can only act
  // on a value already published. `all` because a wake with no waiter is a
  // no-op, so it costs the same and cannot miss a second observer.
  let _ = parkWake(ptrOf(mainDone), true)
  schedTaskExit()
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// bit_rt_spawn / bit_rt_map_pages / bit_rt_unmap_pages — identical role and
// shape to ../linux and ../darwin's own (see their comments for the pin
// rationale: `runtime/gc/mem.bit` reaches these across the module boundary by
// name, ABI.md §9).
//
// #3503: `rtSpawn` below points a spawned task's pc at
// `../../sched/windows/spawn.bit`'s `winSpawnTrampoline`, NOT
// `../../sched/task.bit`'s shared `schedSpawnTrampoline` — the shared one's
// private `taskInvoke` hardcodes the SysV x64 argument register (`rdi`),
// which is wrong for the Win64-compiled `spawn$trampoline$<fid>` it calls
// into. See that file's header for the full root cause and why the fix lives
// there rather than here: it holds the same raw GC-thunk-pointer-across-a-call
// shape `../../sched/task.bit`'s own version does, which `runtime/sched/**`
// is deliberately outside `_tests_/bit/pollfree`'s covered subtrees for (unlike
// `runtime/root/**`, where this file lives).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

export @nosplit fn spawnBindScheduler(schedAddr: int) {
  gSched = schedAddr
}

// A fatal exit for `rtSpawn`'s own error paths ONLY — a live `spawn()` call
// that cannot allocate a task stack or task block, mid-program. NOT
// `bit_rt_os_exit` (ABI.md §19's pinned, stable `(code: i64) -> noreturn`
// export every compiled panic/os.exit() path calls): that symbol belongs to
// `runtime/root/windows/os.bit` (#3332), which owns real process-exit
// semantics on Windows. Defining it here would collide with #3332's own
// definition at final program link (E0042-shaped duplicate), so this stays
// unpinned and unexported.
@nosplit fn winSpawnFatal(code: int) {
  ExitProcess(i32(code))
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_spawn") fn rtSpawn(entry: *byte, arg: int) {
  if (gSched == 0) {
    winSpawnFatal(70)
  }
  let s = taskAt(gSched)
  let stackBase = mapGuardedStack(schedStackBytes)
  if (stackBase == 0) {
    winSpawnFatal(70)
  }
  let taskAddr = mapPages((taskWords + 1) * 8)
  if (taskAddr == 0) {
    winSpawnFatal(70)
  }
  *(taskAt(taskAddr) + taskSpawnEntry) = int(entry)
  schedTaskInit(
    taskAt(taskAddr), stackBase, schedStackBytes,
    entryOf(winSpawnTrampoline), arg,
  )
  schedSpawn(s, taskAddr)
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_map_pages") fn rtMapPages(nbytes: int): int {
  return mapPages(nbytes)
}

export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_unmap_pages") fn rtUnmapPages(addr: int, nbytes: int): int {
  if (unmapPages(addr, nbytes)) {
    return 1
  }
  return 0
}

// Boot the collector and scheduler, run `bit_main`, wait for it, tear down,
// and return the process exit code. THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, mirroring
// ../linux and ../darwin's `boot` with the netpoller/alt-signal-stack/
// BIT_GC_STATS steps removed (see the header) and step 6/7/8's thread
// creation and join done through CreateThread/WaitForSingleObject directly.
fn boot(): int {
  // 1. Fault handling (#3337, ./signal.bit): a process-wide vectored
  //    exception handler, plus this (boot) thread's own stack-overflow
  //    guarantee. Before anything else that can allocate or run user code,
  //    matching where ../linux and ../darwin install their SIGSEGV/SIGBUS
  //    dispositions. No alternate stack to map here (unlike sigaltstack on
  //    the other two platforms) — AddVectoredExceptionHandler runs the
  //    handler on the FAULTING thread's own stack; SetThreadStackGuarantee
  //    is what reserves the headroom it needs (./signal.bit's header).
  winInstallStackGuarantee()
  installFatalSignalHandlers()

  // 1b. Raise the timer tick to 1ms for the whole process (see the extern's
  //     own comment) — once, here, before any worker can park or sleep.
  let _ = timeBeginPeriod(i32(1))

  // 2. Map and bind the heap, then start the collector and type descriptors.
  let spanBytes = arenaWords * 8
  let idxBytes = indexWords * 8
  let span = mapPages(spanBytes)
  let idx = mapPages(idxBytes)
  if (span == 0 || idx == 0) {
    return 70
  }
  rootBindMemory(span, spanBytes, idx, idxBytes, pageSize)
  if (!rootInit(rootEnvMarkStack(gcDefaultMarkStack))) {
    return 70
  }

  // 3. GC config from the environment (BIT_GC/BIT_GC_MIN_KB/BIT_GC_GROWTH_PCT/
  //    BIT_GC_STATS over the defaults), the Bit port of `runtime/root/`'s
  //    `configFromEnv`. `osSetArgs` ran in `winMain` before `boot`, so `envp`
  //    is captured by now — see `winMain`'s own comment for why this MUST
  //    happen before step 2's `rootInit(rootEnvMarkStack(...))` call, which
  //    reads `envp` through `gcEnvValuePtr` (#3391 fault A: that call used to
  //    run before `winCaptureArgsEnv`, reading linear address 0).
  rootConfigureFromEnv(true, false, gcDefaultMinTrigger, gcDefaultGrowthPct, false)

  // 4. Scheduler over the worker blocks.
  let nworkers = rootEnvWorkers(1)
  if (nworkers < 1) {
    nworkers = 1
  }
  if (nworkers > bootMaxWorkers) {
    nworkers = bootMaxWorkers
  }
  let sched: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBlock)
  schedInit(sched, int(ptrOf(workerBlock)), nworkers)
  spawnBindScheduler(int(sched))

  // 4b. Create the netpoller and bind it into the scheduler (#3339) — the
  //     Bit form of `runtime/sched/`'s `Scheduler.init` doing
  //     `poller = try NetPoller.init()` inline, which this port cannot:
  //     `../../sched` may import neither platform's provider, so the
  //     consumer that is already per-platform makes the call, exactly as
  //     ../linux/boottail.bit and ../darwin/boot.bit do at their own step 4b.
  //
  //     A FAILURE IS FATAL, not a degrade-to-no-poller, for the identical
  //     reason ../linux/boottail.bit's step 4b states: silent degradation is
  //     the one outcome that never gets fixed. `pollCreate` already reports a
  //     port handle of 0 as failure — `scPollFd`'s own "no poller bound"
  //     sentinel is 0 — so a single `< 0` test covers both.
  let pollFd = pollCreate()
  if (pollFd < 0) {
    return 70
  }
  bootPollFd = pollFd
  schedSetPollFd(sched, pollFd)

  // 4c. Channel reserve + registry for `bit_rt_chan_make`.
  let chanMem = mapPages(chanReserveBytes)
  if (chanMem == 0) {
    return 70
  }
  chanBootBind(
    int(ptrOf(chanReserveDesc)), chanMem, chanReserveBytes,
    int(ptrOf(chanRegBlock)), int(sched),
  )

  // 4d. Arm stop-the-world.
  stwBind(rootGcAddr(), int(sched), int(ptrOf(chanRegBlock)), rootWorldAddr())

  // 5. Place `bit_main`'s task on worker 0's deque; register before queueing.
  let stackBase = mapGuardedStack(schedMainStackBytes)
  if (stackBase == 0) {
    return 70
  }
  // Published for `vehHandler` (#3337, ./signal.bit) to name a main-stack
  // overflow exactly rather than by heuristic.
  mainStackBaseAddr = stackBase
  let task = int(ptrOf(mainTaskBlock))
  let t: *i64 = ptrOf(mainTaskBlock)
  schedTaskInit(t, stackBase, schedMainStackBytes, entryOf(mainTrampoline), 0)
  if (!schedRegisterBigStack(stackBase, schedMainStackBytes, task)) {
    return 70
  }
  schedRegister(sched, task)
  if (!dqPushBottom(schedWorkerAt(sched, 0) + wkDeque, task)) {
    return 70
  }

  // 6. Bring the workers up on real OS threads via CreateThread directly —
  //    this file's own minimal stand-in for ../../thread/windows (#3335),
  //    which will own the full threadStart/threadRelease contract once it
  //    lands. A failed spawn is not fatal past the first worker (matching
  //    ../linux/../darwin): worker 0 must come up or nothing runs, but a
  //    program should keep the workers it got beyond that.
  let started = 0
  let wi = 0
  while (wi < nworkers) {
    let workerAddr = int(schedWorkerAt(sched, wi))
    let z: *byte = nil
    let argPtr = z + workerAddr
    let flags = STACK_SIZE_PARAM_IS_A_RESERVATION
    let nullAttrs: *byte = nil
    let handle = CreateThread(
      nullAttrs, i64(threadStackBytes),
      entryOf(workerBody), argPtr, flags, ptrOf(winThreadIdScratch),
    )
    *(ptrOf(startHandles) + wi) = int(handle)
    if (int(handle) == 0) {
      if (wi == 0) {
        return 70
      }
      wi = nworkers
    } else {
      started = started + 1
      wi = wi + 1
    }
  }

  // 7. Wait for `main` — `bootWaitForMain` below, bracketed `blocked` exactly
  //    as ../linux/../darwin's boot thread (see their "THE BOOT THREAD AND THE
  //    RENDEZVOUS" header for the full safety argument, which applies
  //    unchanged here: this function allocates nothing on the managed heap at
  //    any point). It was an open-coded 1ms poll here until #3717.
  bootWaitForMain()

  // 8. Signal shutdown, then join each started worker's real OS thread —
  //    WaitForSingleObject on its CreateThread HANDLE, bounded by
  //    `joinTimeoutNs`, natively OS-signaled rather than polled.
  schedShutdown(sched)
  let joinTimeoutMs = i32(joinTimeoutNs / 1000000)
  let ji = 0
  while (ji < started) {
    let h = *(ptrOf(startHandles) + ji)
    let hp: *byte = nil
    // The wait status is discarded on purpose: unlike the POSIX thread
    // providers, CloseHandle below never unmaps the thread's stack — Windows
    // frees it itself once the thread actually exits — so a timed-out wait
    // does not make the following CloseHandle unsafe.
    let _ = WaitForSingleObject(hp + h, joinTimeoutMs)
    let _ = CloseHandle(hp + h)
    ji = ji + 1
  }

  // 8b. Close the netpoller (#3339) — AFTER the join, for the identical
  //     ordering reason ../linux/boottail.bit's own step 8b states: a
  //     worker's loop calls `pollDrain` on this descriptor until it has been
  //     joined, so closing it under a live poller is a use-after-close, not
  //     a leak. The process is about to exit either way; this is here
  //     because the ordering is the part that has to be right if boot is
  //     ever reused (e.g. a stress harness building its own scheduler).
  if (bootPollFd > 0) {
    pollClose(bootPollFd)
    bootPollFd = 0
  }

  // 9. `BIT_GC_STATS`. After the join, so the worker cannot be mid-collection
  //    while the counters are read, and before the exit code goes back to the
  //    entry so it lands on stderr while the process still has one. Same
  //    placement as ../linux/boottail.bit and ../darwin/boot.bit's own step 9.
  if (rootEnvVerbose(false)) {
    statsReport()
  }

  return mainExitCode
}

// The Windows entry point. The PE loader calls this on the Win64 convention
// with no arguments (see the header) once the loader has finished and the
// process is initialized, and calls no epilogue for it — `winMain` itself
// calls `ExitProcess` explicitly rather than returning, since a bare return
// from a CRT-less entry has no defined caller to return into.
//
// `winCaptureArgsEnv()` (./os.bit, #3332) runs FIRST, before `boot()` —
// mirroring ../darwin/boot.bit's `machoMain` and ../linux/boottail.bit's
// `rtStartMain`, which both call `osSetArgs` in the entry function before
// `boot()` ever runs. Windows has no per-process argc/argv/envp handed in at
// entry (see the header), so this file must synthesize that same guarantee
// with an explicit call rather than getting it for free from the loader —
// there is no ordering-neutral place for it inside `boot()`: `boot()`'s own
// step 2 calls `rootInit(rootEnvMarkStack(gcDefaultMarkStack))`, which reads
// `envp` through `gcEnvValuePtr` before any later step in `boot()` would run
// (#3391 fault A — this call used to sit in `boot()`'s step 3, AFTER step 2,
// so that first read dereferenced linear address 0). `winCaptureArgsEnv`
// touches only static module-level arrays and `GetCommandLineW`/
// `GetEnvironmentStringsW` — no heap, no collector, no scheduler — so it has
// no dependency on anything `boot()` sets up and is safe to run first.
export @symbol("_start") fn winMain(): i32 {
  winCaptureArgsEnv()
  let code = boot()
  ExitProcess(i32(code))
  return i32(code) // unreached: ExitProcess does not return.
}
